  "solution": {
    "notes": [
      "A sorting-based solution is also valid, with O(m log m + n log n) time.",
      "Because the problem permits any result order, solutions may return matches in any order.",
      "The hidden expected outputs use a deterministic nums1-order reference serialization for validation."
    ],
    "approach": "Count the frequency of each value in one array, preferably the smaller array. Then scan the other array and emit a value whenever its remaining frequency is positive, decrementing that frequency after each match. This guarantees that each value appears in the result exactly min(count(nums1, value), count(nums2, value)) times.",
    "editorial": {
      "better": {
        "complexity": "O(m log m + n log n) time and O(1) auxiliary space beyond the sorting implementation (or O(log n) recursion stack for typical in-place sorts).",
        "description": "Sort both arrays and use two pointers. When the pointed values are equal, append the value and advance both pointers; otherwise advance the pointer at the smaller value. Sorting makes equal values adjacent and naturally handles duplicates."
      },
      "optimal": {
        "complexity": "O(m + n) average time and O(min(m,n)) average auxiliary space, excluding the output array.",
        "description": "Store frequencies for the smaller array in a hash map. Scan the other array once; when a value still has positive frequency, append it and decrement the frequency. Using the smaller array for the map keeps auxiliary state proportional to min(m,n) on average."
      },
      "bruteForce": {
        "complexity": "O(m*n) time and O(n) auxiliary space for tracking used positions.",
        "description": "For every element in one array, linearly search for an unused equal element in the other array, mark it as used, and append the match. This directly enforces duplicate counts but can repeatedly scan the second array."
      }
    },
    "timeComplexity": "O(m + n) average time with a hash table, where m = nums1.length and n = nums2.length.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(min(m, n)) average auxiliary space for the frequency table and O(min(m, n)) output space in the worst case.",
    "code": {
      "Python3": "class Solution:\n    def intersect(self, nums1: List[int], nums2: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        freq = {}\n        for x in nums2:\n            freq[x] = freq.get(x, 0) + 1\n        result = []\n        for x in nums1:\n            count = freq.get(x, 0)\n            if count > 0:\n                result.append(x)\n                freq[x] = count - 1\n        return result",
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nstatic int* parse_array(const char* s, int* n) {\n    const char* p = s + 1;\n    int cap = 16, size = 0;\n    int* a = (int*)malloc(cap * sizeof(int));\n    while (*p && *p != ']') {\n        if (*p == ',') { ++p; continue; }\n        char* end; long v = strtol(p, &end, 10);\n        if (size == cap) { cap *= 2; a = (int*)realloc(a, cap * sizeof(int)); }\n        a[size++] = (int)v; p = end;\n    }\n    *n = size; return a;\n}\n\nstatic int intersect(const int* nums1, int n1, const int* nums2, int n2, int* out) {\n    int freq[1001] = {0};\n    for (int i=0;i<n2;++i) ++freq[nums2[i]];\n    int m=0;\n    for (int i=0;i<n1;++i) { int x=nums1[i]; if(freq[x]>0){ out[m++]=x; --freq[x]; } }\n    return m;\n}",
      "C++": "class Solution {\npublic:\n    vector<int> intersect(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {\n        unordered_map<int,int> freq;\n        freq.reserve(nums2.size() * 2 + 1);\n        for (int x : nums2) ++freq[x];\n        vector<int> result;\n        result.reserve(min(nums1.size(), nums2.size()));\n        for (int x : nums1) {\n            auto it = freq.find(x);\n            if (it != freq.end() && it->second > 0) {\n                result.push_back(x);\n                --it->second;\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n};",
      "Java": "class Solution {\n    public int[] intersect(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {\n        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int x : nums2) freq.put(x, freq.getOrDefault(x, 0) + 1);\n        int[] out = new int[Math.min(nums1.length, nums2.length)];\n        int size = 0;\n        for (int x : nums1) {\n            int c = freq.getOrDefault(x, 0);\n            if (c > 0) { out[size++] = x; freq.put(x, c - 1); }\n        }\n        return Arrays.copyOf(out, size);\n    }\n}",
      "JavaScript": "const readline = require('readline');\n\nfunction intersect(nums1, nums2) {\n    const freq = new Map();\n    for (const x of nums2) freq.set(x, (freq.get(x) || 0) + 1);\n    const result = [];\n    for (const x of nums1) {\n        const c = freq.get(x) || 0;\n        if (c > 0) { result.push(x); freq.set(x, c - 1); }\n    }\n    return result;\n}"
    }
